  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products in a wide range of categories [10-K line 00134]. This model aims to produce high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover, which enables the company to operate profitably at significantly lower gross margins than most other retailers [10-K line 00134]. The company buys most merchandise directly from suppliers and routes it through cross-docking consolidation points or directly to warehouses [10-K line 00135]. Costco's business model also includes a membership format that encourages frequent shopping, with an annual fee of $65 in the U.S. [10-K line 00134, 00157].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with a wide array of retailers including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190]. The company holds a strong market presence in multiple countries including the U.S., Canada, Mexico, Japan, U.K., Korea, Australia, Taiwan, China, Spain, France, Sweden, Iceland, and New Zealand [10-K line 00131]. It differentiates itself through competitive pricing, quality merchandise, limited SKUs (less than 4,000 active SKUs per warehouse), and its private-label brand, Kirkland Signature [10-K line 00141, 00192].\n\n## Catalysts\nKey catalysts driving Costco’s performance include strong comparable sales growth, increased membership fee revenue, expansion into new markets, and favorable conditions in e-commerce and digital sales [10-K line 00414-00423]. Notably, the company reported a 10% increase in net sales for 2026, driven by comparable sales growth and new warehouse openings [10-K line 00416]. Additionally, higher gasoline prices positively impacted net sales by $3,501 or 130 basis points [10-K line 00454]. The company also benefited from IEEPA tariff refunds, which reduced merchandise costs and were partially reinvested in member value [10-K line 00426].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn 2026, Costco opened 28 new warehouses (including three relocations), resulting in 25 net new warehouses, with 18 in the U.S., five in Canada, and two in Other International segments [10-K line 00415]. Net income increased 14% to $9,226 million, or $20.76 per diluted share, compared to $8,099 million, or $18.21 per diluted share in 2025 [10-K line 00422]. In April 2026, the Board of Directors approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [10-K line 00423]. During the fourth quarter of 2026, Costco reported net income of $2.998 billion, or $6.75 per diluted share, positively impacted by a non-recurring benefit from IEEPA tariff refunds [release line 00020].\n\nThe company’s financial performance was also bolstered by improved operating cash flow and strategic capital spending plans, with projected capital expenditures of approximately $7,500 in fiscal 2027 [10-K line 00513]. Furthermore, Costco continued to invest in its workforce, increasing hourly wages in the U.S. and Canada by $1.00 per hour in March 2026 [10-K line 00185].",
